Refuse a store the start function makes into an unflattened memory.

Patch 0001 refuses a store into a memory it could not flatten only once the
instance is initialized, so that seeding the active segments is not refused.
The start function runs inside instantiation, before that, so its stores were
kept in the interpreter's image; then the first application removed the start
function as evalled, and the image, unflattened, was not written back. A C
module's `_initialize` writes its memory, and what it wrote was lost. A store
the start function makes is refused now too, which fails the instantiation, as
a start function calling an import does: nothing is evalled rather than the
store lost.

diff -ru a/src/tools/wasm-ctor-eval.cpp b/src/tools/wasm-ctor-eval.cpp
--- a/src/tools/wasm-ctor-eval.cpp	2026-10-23 23:12:00.024324703 -0300
+++ b/src/tools/wasm-ctor-eval.cpp	2026-10-23 23:12:11.007894136 -0300
@@ -578,11 +578,14 @@
   }
 
   template<typename T> void doStore(Address address, T value, Name memoryName) {
-    if (instanceInitialized && !flattenedMemory) {
+    if ((instanceInitialized || instance->inStart) && !flattenedMemory) {
       // Nothing would carry this into the module: applyMemoryToModule() needs
       // a single segment at offset 0. Fail this ctor, and only this one.
       // Seeding the active segments at instantiation arrives here too, and
-      // must not be refused -- hence instanceInitialized.
+      // must not be refused -- hence instanceInitialized. The start function
+      // runs before that is set, and is removed as evalled once the state is
+      // applied, so a store it makes is refused too, and fails the
+      // instantiation: no ctor is evalled rather than the store lost.
       throw FailToEvalException("write to unflattened memory");
     }
     Bits::writeLE<T>(value, getMemory(address, memoryName, sizeof(T)));
